How does chronic stress affect the functionality of natural killer cells in the immune system and what is the underlying mechanism responsible for this effect?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","Chronic stress can impair the function of natural killer (NK) cells in the immune system. These cells are critical for rapid responses to infections and the elimination of abnormal cells, such as those with the potential to become cancerous.

1. Chronic stress can lead to a decrease in the number of NK cells in the blood. This can result from the release of stress hormones, such as cortisol, which can suppress the production of NK cells in the bone marrow.

