Thus, 1 mole of H₂SO₄ dissociates into 2 equivalents of H+.
The volume of the solution is 20.0 L, and the desired concentration is 3.00 M.
The total number of moles of H₂SO₄ in the final solution is:
\[ \text{Moles} = \text{Concentration} \times \text{Volume} = 3.00 \text{ M} \times 20.0 \text{ L} = 60.0 \text{ moles} \]
Since H₂SO₄ dissociates into 2 equivalents, the total equivalents in the final solution is:
\[ \text{Equivalents} = \text{Moles} \times 2 = 60.0 \text{ moles} \times 2 = 120 \text{ equivalents} \]
The volume of the final solution is 20.0 L, so the concentration in equivalents is:
