In a dimerization process where a partially resolved chiral substrate reacts to form homochiral (\( RR \) or \( SS \)) and meso (\( RS \) or \( SR \)) dimers, how does the selectivity factor \( s = k_e / k_m \) (where \( k_e \) is the rate constant for homochiral dimer formation and \( k_m \) is the rate constant for meso dimer formation) affect the amplification of the enantiomeric excess (e.e.) of the optically active substance?
</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
The selectivity factor \( s = k_e / k_m \) determines the efficiency of e.e. amplification in the dimerization process:  
1. **Best Case (\( k_m > k_e \), \( s < 1 \))**: When meso dimer formation is favored, the minor enantiomer is preferentially consumed, leading to higher e.e. in the remaining homochiral dimer and recovered monomer.  
2. **Worst Case (\( k_e > k_m \), \( s > 1 \))**: When homochiral dimer formation is favored, the amplification of e.e. is less efficient because the major enantiomer is consumed more rapidly.  
3. **General Case**: Regardless of \( s \), the e.e. of the homochiral dimer (and the monomer recovered from it) will always be higher than the e.e. of the starting monomer. The extent of amplification depends on the magnitude of \( s \).  

Thus, the selectivity factor \( s \) directly influences the degree of e.e. amplification, with lower \( s \) (meso preference) yielding the most effective enrichment.  

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Best case: } s < 1 \ (k_m > k_e) \text{ maximizes e.e. amplification.} \\
&\text{Worst case: } s > 1 \ (k_e > k_m) \text{ minimizes e.e. amplification.} \\
&\text{In all cases, the e.e. of the product is higher than the starting monomer.}
\end{aligned}
}
</GROUND_TRUTH>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The selectivity factor \( s = k_e / k_m \) is the key determinant in the amplification of enantiomeric enrichment (or \(E__M\), which is related to \(E_2\) and \(E_3\) values) in dimerization processes. The higher \(s\), the more efficient the amplification. This is because \(s\) reflects the relative preference of the reaction to form the homochiral dimer over the meso diastereomer. 

The relationship is captured in the equation for \(E__M\) (equilibrium energy) and \(E_2\) (equilibrium energy for the dimer). The equation for \(E_2\) is:  
\[
E_2 = \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{1 -1/s}{ 1 +1/s} \right)
\]  
For example, if \(s = 1\), \(E_2 = 0\), meaning no enrichment.  
\boxed{
\text{The higher } s \text{, the higher } E_2 \text{ (and \(E_M\))}.
}
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

