2. The solution becomes clear, indicating the formation of soluble products.  

The fully ionized equation for the reaction is:  
\[ \text{H(C₇H₅O₂)} (s) + \text{OH}^- (aq) \rightarrow \text{C₇H₅O₂}^- (aq) + \text{H}_2\text{O} (l) \]  

**Explanation:**  
- Benzoic acid is a sparingly soluble weak acid. When NaOH (a strong base) is added, the OH⁻ ions neutralize the H⁺ ions from benzoic acid, forming water.  
- The benzoate ion (C₇H₅O₂⁻) is soluble in water, so the solid dissolves completely.  
- The reaction goes to completion because the weak acid (benzoic acid) is neutralized by the strong base (NaOH), and the products (benzoate ion and water) are stable.  

Thus, the net ionic equation shows the dissolution of the solid and the formation of the soluble benzoate ion.  

\boxed{\text{H(C₇H₅O₂)} (s) + \text{OH}^- (aq) \rightarrow \text{C₇H₅O₂}^- (aq) + \text{H}_2\text{O} (l)}
