$\sin (-\alpha - 180^{\circ}) = \sin[-(180^{\circ} + \alpha)] = -\sin(180^{\circ} + \alpha) = \sin \alpha$, and
$\cos (- \alpha - 180^{\circ}) = \cos[-(180^{\circ} + \alpha)] = \cos(180^{\circ} + \alpha) = -\cos \alpha$.

Then, the original expression can be simplified as follows:
$\dfrac{\cos (180 ^{\circ} + \alpha) \cdot \sin (\alpha + 360 ^{\circ})}{\sin (-\alpha - 180 ^{\circ}) \cdot \cos (-180 ^{\circ} - \alpha)} = \dfrac{-\cos \alpha \cdot \sin \alpha}{\sin \alpha \cdot (-\cos \alpha)} = 1$.

