Use momentum conservation to calculate the approximate recoil velocity of the Earth when a cannonball is fired. The cannonball has a velocity of 90.0 m/s and a mass of approximately 1 kg. The Earth's mass is \(5.97 \times 10^{24}\) kg.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the recoil velocity of the Earth, we use the law of momentum conservation:  
\[ m_1 v_1 + m_2 v_2 = m_1 v_{1_f} + m_2 v_2_f \]  
where \( m_1 \) and \( m_2 \) are the masses of the cannonball and Earth, \( v_1 \) and \( v_2 \) are their initial velocities, and \( v_{1_f} \) and \( v_{2_f} \) are their final velocities.  

The initial velocity of the Earth \( v_2 \) is 0, as it is stationary. The initial velocity of the cannonball \( v_1 \) is \( -90.0 \, \text{m/s} \) (negative because it is directed away from the Earth). The final velocity of the cannonball \( v_{1_f} = 0 \) (since it is moving horizontally).  
The recoil velocity of the Earth is \( v_{2_f} \).  

The equation becomes:  
\[ m_1 (-90.0 \, \text{m/s}) + 1 \times 0 = m_1 (0) + m_2 v_{2_f} \]  
\[ -90.0 \, \text{m/s} \times m_1 = m_2 v_{2_f} \]  
\[ v_{2_f} = \frac -{90.0 \, \text{m/s} \times m_1}}{m_2} \]  
Substituting the given masses:  
\[ v_{2_f} = \frac {-90.0 \, \text{m/s} \times 1 \, \text{kg}}{5.97 \times 10^24 \, \text{kg}} \]  
\[ v_{2f} = \frac {-90.0}{5.97 \times 10^{24}} \, \text{m/s} \]  
\[ v_{2f} \approx -1.63 \times 10^{-22} \, \text{m/s} \]  

The recoil velocity of the Earth is approximately \( -1.63 \times 10^{-22} \, \text{m/s} \).  
The negative sign means the Earth moves in the opposite direction of the cannonball.  

**Final Answer:**  
The approximate recoil velocity of the Earth is \(\boxed{1.63 \times 10^{-22} \, \text{m/s}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

