A spaceship is moving at a speed of 0.6c relative to Earth. Two clocks are on board the ship - one in the cockpit and one in the cargo bay. If the clock in the cockpit shows a time of 3 years when the ship returns to Earth, what will be the time shown on the clock in the cargo bay when it is compared to a clock on Earth? Use the concept of time dilation to calculate the answer.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The time dilation equation states that:  
\[
\Delta{t = \Delta{t_0} \times \sqrt{1 - \frac{v}{2c}}}.
\]  
where:  
- \(\Delta{t}\) is the time elapsed on the ground (in the evening)  
- \(\Delta{t_0}\) is the time elapsed on the moving object (in the cockpit)  
- \( v \) is the velocity of the moving object  
- \( c \) is the speed of flight  

The time dilation equation is:  
\[

\Delta{t = \frac{t_{1}}{1} \times \frac{1}{1 - \frac{v}{c}}}.
\]  
where:  
- \( t_1 \) is the time elapsed on the ground (in the evening)  
- \( t_2 \)) is the time elapsed on the moving object (in the cockpit)  
- \( v \) is the velocity of the moving object  
- \( c \) is the speed of flight  

In this case, the coin is placed on the ground, and the plane is moving at a constant speed \( v \). The time elapsed on the ground is \( t_1 \), and the time elapsed on the plane is \( t_2 \). The time dilation equation is:  
\[
t_1 = \frac{t_2}{1 - \fracv}{c}}
\]  

Thus, the time elapsed on the ground is:  
\[
t_1 = \frac{3}{1 - \frac{0.6}{1}} = \frac{3}{1 - 0.6} = \frac{3}{0.4} = 7.5 \text{ years}.
\]  
The time elapsed on Earth is \(\boxed{7.5 \text{ seconds}}\).
