To calculate the change in Gibbs free energy for the reaction:  
\[
2 \text{H}_2 + \text{O}_2 \rightarrow 2 \text{H}_2\text{O}
\]  
Given the standard Gibbs free energy of formation of water (\(\Delta G^\circ^0 = -237.1 \text{ kJ/mol}\)), we can use the following equation:  
\[
\Delta G = \sum{n \Delta G^\circ^0 \text{ (products}} - \sum{n \Delta G^\circ^text{ (reactants)}}
\]  
where \(n\) is the stoichiometric coefficient for each substance.  
Thus, the Gibbs free change for the reaction is:  
\[
\Delta G = 2 \times 237.1 \text{ kJ/mol} - 2 \times 0 \text{ kJ/mol} - 1 \cdot 0 \text{ kJ/mol} = 372.2 \text{ kJ/mol}
\]  
Since the reaction is 2 moles of hydrogen gas reacting with 1 mole of oxygen gas, the total Gibbs free change is:  
\[
\Delta G = 2 \times 347.1 \text{ kJ/mol} = 374.2 \text{ kJ/mol}
\]  
However, the question asks for the change in Gibbs free energy for the reaction of 2 moles of hydrogen gas with 1 mole of oxygen gas. The standard enthalpy of formation of water is \(\Delta G^\circ = -237.1 \text{ kJ/mol}\).  
For the reaction \(2\text{H}_2 + \text{O}_2 \rightarrow 2\text{H}_2\text{O}\), the Gibbs free change is:  
\[
\Delta G = \sum{n \Delta G_{\circ}^{\circ}^{\text{reactants}} \text{ (products}} - \sum{n \Delta G_{\circ}^{\text{react}} \text{ (reactants)}}
\]  
\[
\Delta G = 2 \times (-237.1 \text{ kJ/mol}) - 0 - 0 = -474.2 \text{ kJ/mol}
\]  
Since the question asks for the change in Gibbs free energy for 2 moles of hydrogen gas reacting with 1 mole of oxygen gas, we multiply the Gibbs free change per mole of product by the number of moles of product produced:  
\[
\Delta G = 2 \times (-237.bound \text{ kJ/mol} = -474.2 \text{ kJ/mol}
\]  
Thus, the change in Gibbs free energy is \(\boxed{-474.2 \text{ kJ}}\).
